  {
    "id": "c080",
    "name": "Reserve Fund",
    "text": "Reserve Fund A contingency reserve fund may be retained for the purpose of covering unexpected cash needs of the Company, if the Manager believes it to be in the best interests of the Company. The amount of this reserve fund, if any, would be established by the Manager, and may be funded by the Manager from any source, including capital raised from the sale of Notes. This reserve fund may be held in cash, bank accounts, certificates of deposit, money market accounts, short-term bankers’ acceptances, publicly traded bond funds or other liquid assets."
  },
  {
    "id": "c081",
    "name": "Sale of Loans Generally",
    "text": "Sale of Loans Generally The Company will make mortgage loans for investment and does not expect to engage in real estate operations in the ordinary course of business {except as may be required if the Company forecloses on a property on which it has invested in a mortgage loan and takes over ownership and management of the property). The Company may also sell non-performing Portfolio Loans or foreclosed property securing Portfolio Loans, or sell an interest in such collateral, to the Servicer or to an affiliate of the Company, the Principals or the Servicer, for the purposes of restructuring the Portfolio Loan or re-positioning the property for sale."
  },
  {
    "id": "c082",
    "name": "Occasional Sale of Mortgage Loans",
    "text": "The Company does not presently intend to invest in mortgage loans primarily for the purpose of reselling such loans in the ordinary course of business; however, the Company may occasionally sell mortgage loans or enter into inter-creditor agreements when the Manager determines that it appears to be advantageous for the Company to do so, based upon then current interest rates, the length of time that the loan has been held by the Company, and the overall investment objectives of the Company."
  },
  {
    "id": "c083",
    "name": "Leveraging the Portfolio",
    "text": "Leveraging the Portfolio If the Manager deems it to be in the best interest of the Company, the Company may borrow funds from a third party lender (which may but is not required to be a bank or other financial institution) in order to fund additional mortgage loans. By leveraging the Company’s loan portfolio, the Company can increase the yield to the Company. This increased yield will result if the interest earned by the Company on its leveraged loans exceeds the interest that must be paid by the Company on the funds borrowed from a third party lender. This “spread” between the interest eared on a leveraged loan and the interest paid on the borrowed funds used to make the loan will accrue to the Company. However, leveraging a Portfolio Loan entails certain risks to the Company that would not otherwise be present if the Company funded all ~15- CONFIDENTIAL of 48 Case3:14-cv-00993-SI Documenti7-1 Filed08/05/14 Pagei24 of 268 of its Portfolio Loans from its own funds. For example, to the extent the Company makes Portfolio Loans at long-term fixed interest rates while it borrows funds at short-term variable interest rates, the Company will be subject to the risk that prevailing interest rates (and the Company’s associated borrowing costs) will rise above the levels earned by the Company on the fixed rate portion of its loan portfolio, causing losses to the Company. (See “Risks and Other Important Factors - Risks of Leverage.”)"
  },
