{
  "id": "c145",
  "name": "Original Issue Discount — Contingent Debt Treatment",
  "text": "Ownership of Notes Original Issue Discount The Company may be deemed for tax purposes to pay to the holders of Notes “additional interest”, as such concept is described in the following discussion, Consequently, the Company will treat the Notes as contingent debt instruments subject to the \"non-contingent bond method\" of reporting interest as set forth in applicable Treasury Regulations. As a result of the classification of the Notes as contingent debt instruments, any gain realized on the sale or exchange of the Notes may be treated as ordinary income, in whole or in part. See “Gain or Loss on Disposition\"."
},
{
  "id": "c146",
  "name": "Comparable Yield",
  "text": "Under the non-contingent bond method, the issuer of a contingent debt instrument must first determine the Comparable Yield. The Comparable Yield is the yield at which the Company could issue a debt instrument with terms and conditions similar to those of the contingent payment debt instrument. The Company has selected 8% as its Comparable Yield, and this rate will be used by the Company for purposes of determining its own taxable income and for any required information reporting. Since there is no direct authority in this area, it is uncertain whether the Internal Revenue Service will agree with this determination and may require a higher or lower Comparative Yield. If the Internal Revenue Service determines that the Comparable Yield should be higher, a Noteholder will be required to accrue the difference between the Comparable Yield selected by the Company and such higher Yield as additional interest income earned each year, even though there are no payments of contingent interest in such year."
},
{
  "id": "c147",
  "name": "Contingent Payment Adjustments",
  "text": "Under the non-contingent bond method, if the interest payable for any period is greater or less than the amount projected, the amount of income included for that period would be either increased or decreased accordingly. Any net increase in the projected contingent payments for a taxable year (e.g., a determination of the amount due pursuant to the additional interest) is treated by a holder as additional interest income."
},
{
  "id": "c148",
  "name": "Comparable Yield Disclaimer",
  "text": "Please note that the Comparable Yield is supplied by the Company solely for computing income under the non-contingent bond method for federal income tax purposes, and does not constitute a projection or representation as to the amounts that Noteholders will actually receive with respect to their Notes."
},
{
  "id": "c149",
  "name": "Gain or Loss on Disposition",
  "text": "Gain or Loss on Disposition If a Note is sold, redeemed or otherwise disposed of, the selling Noteholder will recognize gain or loss equal to the difference between the amount realized from the disposition and the selling Noteholder's adjusted basis in such Note. The adjusted basis generally will equal the cost of such Note to the seller, increased by any original issue discount on such Note included in the seller's income and reduced (but not below zero) by any non-contingent payments on the Note. Because the Notes are characterized as contingent debt instruments under the Treasury Regulations, any gain recognized upon a sale, exchange, retirement, or other disposition of a Note will generally be ordinary income and any loss will be ordinary to the extent of prior ordinary income inclusion."
},
{
