  "name": "Tax-Exempt Entities",
  "text": "Tax-Exempt Entities The income earned by qualified pension, profit-sharing and stock bonus plans, and IRA’s or other taxexempt entities generally is exempt from federal income taxation, except to the extent of “unrelated business taxable income” (UBT) in excess of $1,000 in any year."
},
{
  "id": "c151",
  "name": "Intended UBTI Treatment",
  "text": "As noted above, it is intended that the interest payable with respect to the Notes will be characterized as interest for federal income tax purposes and the Notes will not constitute an equity investment in the Fund, with the result that amounts received with respect to the Notes will not constitute UBTI to the Noteholders."
},
{
  "id": "c152",
  "name": "Equity Recharacterization and UBTI",
  "text": "If the IRS were successfully to characterize the Noteholders as members in the Company, due to the nature of the source of the Fund’s revenues as portfolio interest income, amounts received with respect to the Notes would not be treated as UBTI to the Noteholders. Income to equity investors with respect to Portfolio Loans funded partially by Company Borrowings would constitute “debt financed property” and generate UBTI to the investor. Similarly, income to investors deemed partners with respect to profits to the Company earned from resale or lease of properties acquired by the Company through foreclosure that are encumbered by senior mortgages would also generate UBTI. However, in the past, it has been the practice, although not the obligation, of investment funds sponsored by the Manager’s affiliates to sell defaulting portfolio loans to the Servicer or its affiliates in order to avoid generation of UBTI in connection with possible foreclosures. The Company may continue such practice, although it is not obligated to do so. However, if the Company does continue the practice, it is possible that the IRS will consider the affiliated nature of the Servicer and disregard such sale for purposes of attributing UBTI to investors who are deemed partners,"
