  "id": "c156",
  "name": "ERISA Fiduciary Considerations",
  "text": "Fiduciary Considerations In considering a purchase of the Notes, fiduciaries of Plan Investors should consider their basic fiduciary duty under ERISA that requires them to discharge their investment duties prudently and solely in the interest of plan participants and beneficiaries. In making such a determination, a fiduciary of a Plan Investor should be sure that the investment is in accordance with the governing instruments and the overall policies of the plan, and that the investment will comply with the diversification and prudence requirements of ERISA. Plan Investor fiduciaries should consider the role that a purchase of the Notes would play in the plan’s overail investment portfolio."
},
{
  "id": "c157",
  "name": "ERISA Prohibited Transactions",
  "text": "In addition, provisions of ERISA and the Code prohibit transactions involving the assets of a plan and persons who have specified relationships with a plan (“Parties in Interest” under ERISA and “Disqualified Persons” under the Cade, collectively referred to herein as “Parties in Interesf’), unless an exemption is available for such transaction. A Plan Investor fiduciary should be sure that purchase of Notes will not constitute or give rise to a direct or indirect non-exempt prohibited transaction. In particular, Plan Investors must make an independent investment decision with respect to their purchase of Notes issued by the Company and must not rely upon the Manager or its affiliates for investment advice regarding such participation."
},
{
  "id": "c158",
  "name": "ERISA Scope Disclaimer",
  "text": "ERISA and its accompanying regulations are complex and, to a great extent, have not yet been interpreted by the courts or the administrative agencies. This discussion only addresses certain features of ERISA as it applies to the Notes, and does not purport to constitute a thorough analysis of ERISA with respect to your investment in the Company."
},
{
  "id": "c159",
  "name": "ERISA Counsel Consultation",
  "text": "EACH INVESTOR SUBJECT TO ERISA SHOULD CONSULT WITH ITS OWN LEGAL COUNSEL CONCERNING THE IMPLICATIONS UNDER ERISA OF THE OWNERSHIP OF NOTES."
},
{
  "id": "c160",
  "name": "Investment Company Act Status",
  "text": "CERTAIN OTHER REGULATORY CONSIDERATIONS The operation of the Company and the ownership of the Notes by Noteholders will be structured so as not to subject the Fund to the provisions of the Investment Company Act of 1940 (the “Znvestment Company Acf’). In the alternative, the Company expects to rely, among other things, on the exemption from registration afforded by compliance with Section 3(c)(5) of the Investment Company Act. Section 3(c)(5) excludes from the definition of “investment company” issuers of non-redeemable securities engaged in “purchasing or otherwise acquiring mortgages and other liens on and interests in real estate”."
},
{
  "id": "c161",
  "name": "Securities Act Exemption and Resale Restriction",
  "text": "The Notes are being issued without registration under the Securities Act of 1933 (the “Securities Act’) by reason of the exemptions from the registration requirements for private offerings set forth in Section 4(2) and Rule 506 of Regulation D of the Securities Act. As such, the Notes cannot be resold except pursuant to registration under the Securities Act or an exemption therefrom."
},
{
  "id": "c162",
  "name": "State Securities Laws",
  "text": "In addition, the Notes are being issued pursuant to exemptions or permits under applicable state securities laws, and cannot be resold except in compliance with restrictions imposed by such laws."
},
{
  "id": "c163",
  "name": "Status of Noteholders and Company as Lenders",
  "text": "CONSIDERATIONS RELATING TO STATUS OF NOTEHOLDERS AND COMPANY AS LENDERS The Company is a lender with respect to its Portfolio Loans, and, for certain purposes, the Company may be deemed a borrower and the Noteholders may be deemed lenders with respect to the Notes. California and other states have numerous laws and regulations which apply to the activities of lenders and the rights of borrowers. The applicability of these laws and regulations, and their exemptions and exclusions, are frequently complex and highly fact-specific, and they vary by jurisdiction and are subject to change. In addition, litigation in a number of states has imposed liability upon lenders, or otherwise adversely impacted lenders, in ways to which Noteholders may not be accustomed as a result of other investment activities."
},
{
  "id": "c164",
  "name": "Usury",
  "text": "Usury A number of states have adopted usury laws, which generally prohibit the charging of interest in certain circumstances in excess of a statutorily-defined rate. The rates of interest to be paid to the Noteholders by the Company, and the rates of interest to be paid by the Company’s Portfolio Borrowers to the Company, may exceed the applicable statutorily-defined usury rates. As of November 2007, charging interest at a rate greater than ten percent (10%) per annum is usurious in California unless an exemption or exclusion therefrom exists, but such threshold rate may change in the future in California or rates in other jurisdictions may be applicable. As a result, were any of the capital loans made by the Noteholders to the Company or by the Company to the Portfolio Borrowers to be challenged, as usurious, in certain circumstances, such as in a bankruptcy, it is possible other creditors, a bankruptcy trustee or receiver or another interested party (an “Interested Party”) would be able to successfully invalidate all or a portion of the interest due to the Noteholders or to the Company. This could result in a court imposing treble damages or other penalties and fines, including criminal penalties."
},
{
  "id": "c165",
  "name": "Compliance with Lending Laws",
  "text": "The Company intends to rely on qualified advisors and use commercially reasonable efforts to comply with laws and regulations applying to lenders and borrowers, and to seek exemptions and exclusions as advisable from such laws where appropriate to meet the investment objectives of the Company and this offering,"
},
{
  "id": "c166",
  "name": "Lending Laws and Licensing Risk",
  "text": "Lending Laws The California Finance Lenders Law and similar statues in other states may permit a borrower or a person acting on behalf of a borrower to avoid paying principal and interest on any loan made by a person required to be licensed under such a statute who does not become so registered. Any Noteholder that is not properly licensed as a finance lender in a jurisdiction which requires such licensing in connection with its loans to the Company may risk having its ability to collect principal and interest from the Company challenged by an Interested Party in any adversarial proceeding relating to the Company, such as bankruptcy. In addition, the Company itself may be subject to such risk in connection with its Loans to the Portfolio Borrowers."
},
{
  "id": "c167",
  "name": "Finance-Lender Licensing and Compliance",
  "text": "The Company expects to make its Portfolio Loans pursuant to a finance lender’s license issued by California and other jurisdictions as applicable. The Company also intends to rely on qualified advisors and use commercially reasonable efforts to comply with laws and regulations applying to lenders and borrowers, and to seek exemptions and exclusions as advisable from such laws where appropriate to meet the investment objectives of the Company and this offering. The Manager anticipates that such efforts -3l- CONFIDENTIAL Case: 12-04137 Doc# 75-2 Filed: 09/16/13 Entered: 09/16/13 15:56:26 Page 3/ of 48 should be sufficient to avoid issues of noncompliance. However, Noteholders should be aware, that, under certain circumstances, a failure to comply with applicable regulations by the Company or a Noteholder could result in civil or criminal penalties."
},
{
  "id": "c168",
  "name": "Lender Liability",
  "text": "Lender Liability As an additional consideration, legal decisions in many jurisdictions, including California, have imposed liability upon lenders for actions such as declaring defaults with respect to loans and refusing to meet Company loan commitments under certain circumstances. In addition, some courts have permitted litigants to pursue claims against lenders for environmental torts of a borrower and other liability as a result of their association with a borrower. Such so-called “lender liability” is a developing and uncertain area of the law, and there can be no assurance that such a claim could not be brought against the Company or, by extension, a Noteholder. In addition, in some cases, courts have recharacterized loans or debt securities as equity instruments, such that lenders or debt security holders have been subject to “equitable subordination” and thus not entitled to the preferred status of a creditor in a bankruptcy or other adversarial proceeding. Such decisions have been highly fact specific, and there can be no assurance that a court would not follow a similar approach with respect to the Noteholders’ loans to the Company, or the Company’s loans to its Portfolio Borrowers."
},
{
  "id": "c169",
  "name": "Legal Counsel Consultation on Lender Issues",
  "text": "Noteholders are encouraged to consult with their legal counsel regarding the lender-related issues discussed above."
},
{
  "id": "c170",
  "name": "VII. Risk Factors — General Warning",
  "text": "VII. Risk Factors The purchase of Notes will involve a high degree of risk. By its nature a summary of all possible risks is incomplete. However, among other factors, prospective Noteholders are urged to consider the following:"
},
{
  "id": "c171",
  "name": "Reliance on Portfolio Borrower Management",
  "text": "Risk Inherent in Relying on Management by Portfolio Borrower: Typically a Portfolio Borrower will be structured as a single purpose entity or venture for the purpose of acquiring, developing or holding the real property (a “Projecf’) collateralizing a Portfolio Loan. The Portfolio Borrower and the Project will be managed exclusively by the principals of the Portfolio Borrower or its co-investors or affiliates."

  },
  {
    "id": "c172",
    "name": "No Voice in Portfolio Borrower Management",
    "text": "No Voice in Portfolio Borrower Management, Noteholders will have no opportunity to directly affect the management of the Portfolio Borrower or of the Project. The Company is also likely to have little opportunity to influence the management of the Portfolio Borrower or Project, except to the limited extent of imposing conditions to the draw of funds under a Portfolio Loan. Therefore, the successful operation of the Portfolio Borrower, the choice and terms of any senior Project financing, the success of the Project, the repayment of a Portfolio Loan, and the ability of the Portfolio Borrower to repay the Portfolio Loan will depend greatly upon the skill, experience and efforts of the Portfolio Borrower in the acquisition, development, management, improvement and sale of the Project and in the management of the Portfolio Borrower. In the event the Project is not successfully completed, in addition to the inability of the Portfolio Manager to repay such Loan, the value of the Loan collateral is likely to be impaired."
  },
  {
    "id": "c173",
    "name": "No Prior History",
    "text": "No Prior History. Although a Portfolio Borrower or its principals may be experienced real estate investors, the Project may have no operating or financial history. The prior successes of the principals of the Portfolio Borrower in connection with previous real estate investments are no assurance that the Portfolio Borrower will enjoy comparable success with respect to the Project collateralizing the Portfolio Loan."
  },
  {
    "id": "c174",
    "name": "Effect of Portfolio Borrower Performance",
    "text": "if a Portfolio Loan or Project collateral is impaired by the poor performance of a Portfolio Borrower, the Company’s ability to repay the Notes may be impaired."
  },
  {
    "id": "c175",
    "name": "Risks in Portfolio Borrower’s Real-Property Investment",
    "text": "Risk Inherent in the Portfolio Borrower’s Investment in Real Property: As noted, the Company’s ability to repay the Principal and Interest on the Notes will depend upon the Portfolio Borrower’s ability to manage the Project and to repay the Portfolio Loan, which ultimately depends upon the successful acquisition, management, operation and disposition by the Portfolio Borrower of the Project. In addition to the risks of poor management noted above, certain risks inherent in the operation of real property in general, or of a Project in particular, may adversely affect the ability of the Portfolio Borrower to repay the Portfolio Loan or may impair the value of the Project as collateral for the repayment of the Portfolio Loan. Such risks include, among others, a loss to the Project occasioned by the following:"
  },
  {
    "id": "c176",
    "name": "Changes in Economic Conditions",
    "text": "e Changes in Economic Conditions. The Portfolio Borrower’s investment in the Project will be subject to the risks generally incident to the ownership of real property, such as adverse changes in regional or local economic conditions, including local employment, interest rates, inflation, availability of funds, governmental rules and fiscal policies, existing competition and potential competition generated out of competing projects being built, leased or operated in the same general area as the Project. Additionally, national economic changes and international events may adversely impact general conditions and the economy in the market area of the Project."
  },
  {
    "id": "c177",
    "name": "Increased Costs of Development or Operations",
    "text": "e Increased Costs of Development or Operations. The cost of planned development or operations of the Project may exceed estimates, or may not be permissible under then-existing laws, ordinances, regulations and building codes."
  },
  {
    "id": "c178",
    "name": "Environmental Concerns",
    "text": "Environmental Concerns. Under various federal, state and local laws, ordinances and regulations, an owner of real estate may be liable for the costs of clean-up of certain conditions relating to the presence of hazardous or toxic materials on, in or emanating from the Project, and any related damages to natural resources. Such laws often impose liability without regard to whether the owner knew of, or was responsible for, the presence of hazardous or toxic materials."
  },
  {
    "id": "c179",
    "name": "Terms of Senior Financing",
    "text": "Terms of Senior Financing. While leveraged investments offer the opportunity for capital appreciation, such investments also involve a higher degree of risk. The Portfolio Borrower's financing of a Project may involve varying degrees of leverage in order to provide greater financial return to the Portfolio Borrower, as a result of which recessions, operating problems and other general business and economic risks may have a more pronounced effect on the profitability or survival of such investments than if leverage were not employed. The Portfolio Borrower may also have to make debt service payments, including interest, which will most likely fluctuate based on a spread above an underlying base rate. By its very nature, a variable interest rate will move up or down based on changes in the economy and other factors, all of which are beyond the control of the Portfolio Borrower. Accordingly, there can be no assurance that such rates will not rise significantly and, consequently, that a Portfolio Borrower would be required to pay more interest than is anticipated with respect to its senior financing on the Project, thereby impairing its ability to repay the Portfolio Loan."
  },
  {
    "id": "c180",
    "name": "Foreclosure by Senior Lender",
    "text": "e Foreclosure by Senior Lender. The Company may suffer a loss of its Portfolio Loan upon a foreclosure of the Project by a senior Iender. The loans made to the Portfolio Borrower in connection with the acquisition and development of the Project may be secured by first and second mortgages on the Project. Incurring mortgage indebtedness increases the risk of loss to the Portfolio Borrower because the debt service must be paid. If there is a payment or other default on these senior loans, the lender may foreclose on the Project and the Project Owner will not have the capacity to repay all or a portion of the Portfolio Loans. The value of the Project in foreciosure may be insufficient collateral to repay in full both the senior loan and the Portfolio Loan."
  },
  {
    "id": "c181",
    "name": "Mechanics Liens",
    "text": "Mechanics Liens. The Project may be subject to a mechanic’s lien which entitles the holder of such lien to foreclose on the Project. State law provides any person who supplies services or materials to a real estate project may impose a lien against the project securing any amounts owed to such person. Although the Portfolio Borrower may be required by the terms of the Portfolio Loan to utilize procedures to prevent the occurrence of mechanics’ liens (such as requiring mechanic lien releases prior to payment and issuing joint-party checks) no assurance can be given that mechanics’ liens will not appear against the Project. If a mechanic’s lien does appear, then it must be negotiated by the Portfolio Borrower in order to obtain its release or the person holding such lien will have the right to bring an action to foreclose on the Project in order to satisfy the amount due under the Hien."
  },
  {
    "id": "c182",
    "name": "Eminent Domain",
    "text": "s Eminent Domain. The Project or a portion of the Project could become subject to an eminent domain or inverse condemnation action. Such an action could have a material adverse effect on the operations or marketability of the Project, and, as a consequence, adversely affect the ability of the Portfolio Borrower to repay the Loan. In addition, the value of the Project as coilateral would be impaired and the Project may have insufficient remaining value to repay accrued Interest and Principal on foreclosure."
  },
  {
    "id": "c183",
    "name": "Risks in Company Debt Investments",
    "text": "Risks Inherent in the Company’s Investment in Debt Instruments. The Company’s funding of Portfolio Loans presents substantial risk to its capital that may affect its ability to repay Principal and Interest of the Notes, including risks that are inherent in the business of real estate lending. The CONFIDENTIAL of 48 Company, for instance, may be limited by law or by the terms of its loan agreement, including without limitation with respect to the following:"
  },
  {
    "id": "c184",
    "name": "Effect of Portfolio Diversification",
    "text": "Effect on Risk of Portfolio Diversification. The financial failure of a Portfolio Borrower and default on a Portfolio Loan could result in a significant reduction in the Company’s income. The Company will be relying solely on the value of the underlying collateral as security for the Portfolio Loan in such event. With respect to the collateral, the Company will be subject to general economic risks with respect to its debt investments similar to those experienced by the Portfolio Borrower with respect to the Project. Accordingly, the diversification of the Company’s Portfolio Loans with respect to borrowers, principal amounts, location and nature of collateral, and loan seniority will have a substantial effect on the Loan Portfolio’s risk profile."
  },
  {
    "id": "c185",
    "name": "Lender Remedies",
    "text": "Lender Remedies. Portfolio Loans will generally not be personal obligations of the applicable borrowers. In the event of a borrower default under a Portfolio Loan, the Company may be entitled to foreclose upon the property securing the Portfolio Loan. A foreclosure action or other lender remedies may be subject to delays and additional expenses if defenses or counterclaims are interposed, and may require several years to complete. Moreover, a non-collusive, regularly conducted foreclosure sale may be challenged as a fraudulent conveyance, regardless of the parties’ intent, if a court determines that the sale was for less than fair consideration and such sale occurred when the borrower was insolvent and within one year (or within the applicable state statute of limitations if the trustee in bankruptcy elects to proceed under state fraudulent conveyance laws) of the filing of bankruptcy. Similarly, a suit against a borrower on a note may take several years, and generally is a remedy alternative to foreclosure so that the Company may be precluded from pursuing both foreclosure and an action on a note simultaneously."
  },
  {
    "id": "c186",
    "name": "Cross-Collateralization of Loans",
    "text": "Cross-Collateralization of Loans. The repayment of one or more of the Portfolio Loans may be cross-coliateralized by a direct or indirect interest in more than one Project. A Project will be released as collateral as it is sold and the payment is made on the Portfolio Loan. However, there is no assurance that Projects will be sold in the order of their coilateral value, or that the passage of time or changes in market conditions will preserve the value of remaining collateral. Therefore, the remaining Project may be insufficient collateral to provide protection in the event that the Portfolio Loan ceases to perform."
  },
  {
    "id": "c187",
    "name": "Inadequate Preservation of Collateral",
    "text": "Inadequate Preservation of Collateral. Although the Company intends to require its Portfolio Borrowers to maintain adequate insurance coverage against liability for personal injury and property damage, such insurance may prove insufficient to cover any liabilities or casualty losses incurred by a Portfolio Borrower. Also certain risks may be uninsurable or may become uninsurable, or may become insurable only at prohibitive cost, such as the risk of property damage and general liability from earthquakes, floods, damage from terrorist activities, and/or certain environmental hazards. In addition, such risks may be insurable only in amounts that are less than the full market value or replacement cost of the relevant collateral. In addition to the unintentional loss of the collateral, the Portfolio Borrower may permit uninsured waste or other damage to the collateral by tenants, licensees or invitees. In the event of the occurrence of such risk or waste, the collateral may be substantially impaired or destroyed, and the potential loss to the Company in the event the applicable Portfolio Loan does not perform could be substantial."
  },
  {
    "id": "c188",
    "name": "Subordinated Loan Positions",
    "text": "Subordinated Loan Positions. The Company’s Portfolio Loans may carry additional risks typical of subordinate loan positions, including subordinate positions to entities affiliated with the Manager, Other Sponsored Funds or other private investors working with the Manager or its affiliates. Where a Portfolio Borrower defaults under a mezzanine investment comprising a subordinate note in a loan evidenced by both a senior note and a subordinate note and secured by CONFIDENTIAL -35-~ Case: 12-04137 Doc#75-2 Filed: 09/16/13 Entered: 09/16/13 15:56:26” Page 41 of 48 a single mortgage lien encumbering the Portfolio Borrower’s real property, the Company may seek an inter-creditor agreement to provide the Company with the right to cure the Portfolio Borrower's defaults. Should such an inter-creditor agreement be in place, the Company would expect (a) to realize the value of any remaining equity in the collateral once the senior lender has completed foreclosure, or (b) provided sufficient funds are then available, to cure the Portfolio Borrower’s default with respect to the senior notes, buy the senior notes and/or foreclose the mortgage lien. In either case, the Company’s remedies will be subject to the rights of the senior lenders. Additionally, in order to purchase senior notes, the Company may need to obtain financing, either through capital raised from Noteholders or new investors, Company Borrowings, or through third-party financing, which may not be available."
  },
  {
    "id": "c189",
    "name": "Subordinated Loan with Equity Position Collateral",
    "text": "Subordinated Loan with Equity Position Collateral. Should a Portfolio Loan be secured by a pledge of equity interests in a Portfolio Borrower, but be subordinate to a third party mortgage loan made simultaneously to such Borrower, the Company may be able to exercise its remedies independently of the senior lender’s remedies. However, if the Company forecloses on its equity pledge, the Company will then own the Portfolio Borrower, and become obligated for mortgage liabilities to the senior lender and other liabilities of a failing borrower. Additionally, the Company may be subject to equitable defenses to foreclosure on its loan collateral on the basis of its equity interests in the Portfolio Borrower."
  },
  {
    "id": "c190",
    "name": "Laws Regulating Lenders",
    "text": "Laws Regulating Lenders. In connection with the Portfolio Loans, the Company intends to use commercially reasonable efforts in reliance on qualified advisors, including its affiliate Bar-K, Inc., to comply with laws and regulations applying to lenders in the relevant jurisdictions. However, such laws are not always clearly applicable to a particular foan transaction or investment structure, or they may change. In such event, the Company’s interest in a Portfolio Loan may be materially impaired, with the consequence that the Company’s ability to repay Principal or Interest on the Notes may be materially impaired."
  },
  {
    "id": "c191",
    "name": "Usury Risk for Portfolio Loans",
    "text": "Usury. The structure of the Company’s Portfolio Loans is expected to comply with California usury laws and the laws of other relevant jurisdictions. However, usury laws and their exemptions are complex and may change. If, despite the Company’s reasonable efforts in reliance on qualified advisors to avoid such a result, a Portfolio Loan made by the Company does not comply with applicable state usury laws, the Company may be responsible for payment of penalties and face the potential loss of its Portfolio Loan investment."
  },
  {
    "id": "c192",
    "name": "Conflicts between Company and Manager",
    "text": "Risks Resulting from Conflicts of Interest between the Company and the Manager and its Principals and Affiliates: The Manager, its affiliates and their principals, including the Principals, may have direct and indirect equity and compensation interests with respect to the Portfolio Loans, as well as in co-investors and in co-lenders with the Company, that are likely to create a conflict of interest between the investment interests of the Company and the investment interests of the Manager, its affiliates and principals. Risks and other considerations inherent in this context that may affect the Company’s ability to pay the Notes when due include, but are not limited to, the following:"
  },
  {
    "id": "c193",
    "name": "Time Demands",
    "text": "Time Demands. The Manager or the Principals will also pursue investment and lending activities with R.E. Loans and Other Sponsored Funds, as well as for private investors with whom they may choose to do business. In addition, Principals may have commitments in connection with the management of affiliates of the Company or Manager. Although the Manager anticipates that they will have sufficient resources and time to commit to the management of the Company for the CONFIDENTIAL Case: 12-04137 Doc# 75-2 Filed: o9/i6/13 Entered: 09/16/13 15:56:26 Page 42 of 48 benefit of the Noteholders, their interests and activities in connection with such other investments may create a conflict with the Company’s interests."
  },
  {
    "id": "c194",
    "name": "Proportionally Disproportionate Interest of Manager",
    "text": "Proportionally Disproportionate Interest of Manager. The Company’s Manager and its members will receive Company gains and certain other income that, relative to their capital contribution to the Company, are proportionally greater than the Interest payable to Noteholders on account of their investment in the Notes, Accordingly, the Manager’s risk profile with respect to the use of the Company’s capital may diverge from that of a Noteholder."
  },
  {
    "id": "c195",
    "name": "Potential Conflicts of Interest",
    "text": "Potential Conflicts of Interest. The Manager may encounter various potential and actual conflicts of interest between itself and the Company as a function of various activities including, but not limited to, the provision by its affiliates or Principals of certain management or other real estate related services to the Portfolio Borrower or its affiliates. Also, the Manager, its Principals or affiliates may encounter various conflicts of interest with the Company in the event that they or an Other Sponsored Fund or private investor with whom they are doing business co-invest with the Company or purchase or sell loans from or to the Company."
  },
  {
    "id": "c196",
    "name": "Entrustment of Management",
    "text": "Accordingly, no person should purchase Notes from the Company unless he is willing to entrust all aspects of the management, financing, and development of a Project to the Portfolio Borrower, and management of the Company, valuation of the Projects and the terms of the Portfolio Loans to the Manager."
  },
  {
    "id": "c197",
    "name": "Private Equity Investment Risks",
    "text": "Risks Inherent in Private Equity Investments: In addition to risks associated with investments in real property related assets, the purchase of Notes from the Company will also be subject to certain risks that are typical of investments in private equity funds, as well as risks which are more particular to the Company, including the following:"
  },
  {
    "id": "c198",
    "name": "No Operating History",
    "text": "No Operating History. The Company will begin operations upon closing. It has no operating history and has not identified all of its portfolio loans. Although the Principals and affiliates of the Manager and their sponsored investment vehicles may have achieved favorable returns with some of their previous loan investments, the performance of past investments cannot be relied upon to predict the Company’s success."
  },
  {
    "id": "c199",
    "name": "Concentration of Loan Investments",
    "text": "Concentration af Loan Investments. Depending upon the amount of capital committed by prospective Noteholders, the Company’s portfolio may become concentrated in a limited number of portfolio loans, increasing the vulnerability of the portfolio as compared with a portfolio that is more diversified in the number and location of portfolio loans and Portfolio Borrowers."
  },
  {
    "id": "c200",
    "name": "Unspecified Investments",
    "text": "Unspecified Investments. A Notehoider must rely upon the ability of the Manager to identify, structure and implement portfolio loans consistent with the Company’s investment objective. There can be no assurance that loans of a suitable nature will be available on the market. It is possible that the Company will never be fully invested if sufficiently attractive loan opportunities are not identified."
  },
  {
    "id": "c201",
    "name": "Failure of Security",
    "text": "Failure of Security. Although the Company’s repayment of the Notes will be secured by an interest in the assets of the Company, such security interest may be subordinated to senior security interests in connection with possible Company Borrowings, as well as to statutorily protected interests, such as liens in connection with unpaid taxes. The terms of Company CONFIDENTIAL ~ 37 - of 48 Borrowings may also impose certain conditions on subordinate lenders with respect to the exercise of remedies in the event the Company is in default under the Company Borrowing, Therefore, there is no assurance that the Noteholders’ security interest will be perfected in seniority over the interests of other creditors of the Company in such assets, that its remedies will be enforceable, or if such collateral is foreclosed upon, that it will be sufficient to repay all outstanding Principal and Interest on the Notes."
  },
  {
    "id": "c202",
    "name": "Restrictions on Transferability",
    "text": "e Restrictions on Transferability. The transferability of the Notes will be severely restricted by the Note Purchase Agreement, the terms of the Note and by federal and state securities laws."
  },
  {
    "id": "c203",
    "name": "No Market; Illiquidity of Notes",
    "text": "No Market; illiquidity of Notes, An investment in the Notes will be illiquid and involves a high degree of risk. There is no public market for the Notes, and it is not expected that a public market will develop. Consequently, Noteholders will bear the economic risks of their investment until the Notes are paid in full. Prospective Noteholders will be required to represent and agree that they are purchasing the Notes for their own account for investment only and not with a view to the resale or distribution thereof."
  },
  {
    "id": "c204",
    "name": "Passive Investment",
    "text": "Passive investment. The structure of a Noteholder’s investment in the Notes precludes the Noteholders from active participation in the Company’s investment lending decisions or from voting on Company operating matters. Moreover, the Noteholders will not be permitted to evaluate Portfolio Loan opportunities or relevant business, economic, financial or other information that will be used by the Manager in making decisions."
  },
  {
    "id": "c205",
    "name": "Dependence on Management Team",
    "text": "Dependence on Management Team. Decisions with respect to the management of the Company will be made by the Principals. The success of the Company will depend on the ability of the Principals to identify and consummate suitable Portfolio Loans, and to improve the performance of such Portfolio Loans. The loss of the services of one or more of the Principals could have a material adverse impact on the Company’s ability to realize its investment objectives. Further, certain of such Principals may have significant business commitments to other companies or activities, including to R.E. Loans and Other Sponsored Investment Funds, that will occupy some portion of his business time. Additional persons may be admitted as principals of the Manager following the Company’s initial closing, and the Noteholders will have no power to prevent any specific person from being admitted to the Manager’s management team for the Company."
  },
  {
    "id": "c206",
    "name": "Tax, ERISA and Other Legal Matters",
    "text": "Tax, ERISA and Other Legal Matters. There are risks associated with the tax aspects of an investment in the Notes and of the Company’s Loans that are complex and will not be the same for all prospective Notehoiders. Also, some Notehoiders, depending upon their state of residence, may be subject to certain laws and regulations governing the lending of money. Similarly, payment of the Notes to Noteholders who are not California residents may, depending upon their state of residence, be subject to certain defenses by the Company or interested persons claiming through the Company under applicable usury laws. In addition, there may be special concerns for investors subject to ERISA and any rules or regulations promulgated thereunder. Please review Section VI Tax and Legal Considerations of this Memorandum, Each proposed investor is advised to consult its own legal, tax and ERISA advisors before purchasing Notes."
  },
  {
    "id": "c207",
    "name": "Company Not Registered",
    "text": "Company Not Registered. The Manager believes the nature of the Company will not subject it to the registration requirements of the United States Investment Company Act of 1940, as amended {the “Company Act”). There is no assurance that the Manager’s belief in this regard will continue to be correct. Due to the various burdens of compliance with the Company Act, the performance of the Company and its ability to repay Principal and Interest of the Notes could be adversely impacted if the Company becomes subject to the Company Act, Neither the Company - 38 - CONFIDENTIAL of 48 nor its counsel can assure investors that, under certain conditions, changing circumstances, or changes in the law, the Company may not become subject to the Company Act or other burdensome regulation."
  },
  {
    "id": "c208",
    "name": "No Assurance of Repayment",
    "text": "In conclusion, despite the reasonable and good faith efforts of the Principals, their extensive experience in mortgage lending and their favorable track record with making loans similar to those targeted for the Company, the Company cannot provide assurance that Portfolio Loans will perform as expected. Accordingly, there is no assurance that the Company will be able to generate returns to pay all Principal and accrued Interest on the Notes to the Noteholders. An investment in the Notes should only be considered by investors who can reasonably afford a loss of their entire investment."
  },
  {
    "id": "c209",
    "name": "VIII. Suitable Investors — Eligibility and Subscription",
    "text": "VIII. Suitable Investors In order to purchase Notes, an investor must meet certain eligibility and suitability standards and must execute a Subscription Agreement in the form attached hereto as Appendix B. By executing the Subscription Agreement, an investor makes certain representations and warranties, upon which the Manager will rely in accepting subscriptions. Please read the Subscription Agreement carefully. In addition, please note that in order to make additional subscriptions to increase the principal amount of a Note or in connection with an Interest Roll-over Request, a Noteholder will also need to meet the eligibility and suitability standards at the time of the subscription for such increase or such Request."
  },
  {
    "id": "c210",
    "name": "Accredited Investor Requirement",
    "text": "Each investor must represent in writing, among other representations contained in the Subscription Agreement, as follows: 1. That the investor is an “Accredited Investor” (within the meaning of Rule 501 promulgated under the Securities Act), by virtue of the truth of at least one of the following:"
  },
  {
    "id": "c211",
    "name": "1(i) Accredited Individual",
    "text": "(i) The investor is an INDIVIDUAL who has a net worth, either individually or upon a joint basis with the Investor’s spouse, of at least $1,000,000 (within the meaning of such terms as used in the definition of “Accredited Investor” contained in Rule 501 promulgated under the Securities Act), or has had an individual income in excess of $200,000 for each of the two most recent years, or a joint income with the Investor’s spouse in excess of $300,000 in each of those years, and has a reasonable expectation of reaching the same income level in the current year; or"
  },
  {
    "id": "c212",
    "name": "1(ii) Accredited Irrevocable Trust",
    "text": "(ii) The investor is an IRREVOCABLE TRUST, with total assets in excess of $5,000,000 whose purchase is directed by a person with such knowledge and experience in financial and business matters that such person is capable of evaluating the merits and risks of the prospective investment."
  },
  {
    "id": "c213",
    "name": "1(iii) Accredited Financial or Institutional Entity",
    "text": "(iii) The investor is a BANK, INSURANCE COMPANY, INVESTMENT COMPANY registered under the United States Companies Act of 1940, as amended (the “Companies Act”), a broker or dealer registered pursuant to Section 15 of the United States Securities Exchange Act of 1934, as amended, a business development company, a Small Business Investment Company licensed by the United States Small Business Administration, a plan with total assets in excess of $5,000,000 established and maintained by a state for the benefit of its employees, or a private business development company as defined in Section 202(a)(22) of the United States Investment Advisers Act of 1940, as amended;"
  },
  {
    "id": "c214",
    "name": "1(iv) Accredited Employee Benefit Plan",
    "text": "(iv) The investor is an EMPLOYEE BENEFIT PLAN and gither (A) all investment decisions are made by a bank, savings and loan association, insurance company, or registered investment advisor, or (B) the Investor has total assets in excess of $5,000,000 or, if such plan is a self-directed plan, investment decisions are made solely by persons who are accredited investors; or"
  },
  {
    "id": "c215",
    "name": "1(v) Accredited Business Entity",
    "text": "{v) The investor is a CORPORATION, PARTNERSHIP, LIMITED LIABILITY COMPANY OR BUSINESS TRUST, not formed for the purpose of acquiring the Note, or an organization described in Section 501(c)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, as amended (the “Cede”), in each case with total assets in excess of $5,000,000; or"
  },
  {
    "id": "c216",
    "name": "1(vi) Accredited Look-Through Entity or Trust",
    "text": "(vi) The investor is an ENTITY in which all of the equity owners, or a living trust or other revocable trust in which ail of the grantors and trustees, qualify under clause (1), (ii), Gii), (iv) or (v) above or this clause (vi); and"
  },
  {
    "id": "c217",
    "name": "2. Entity Beneficial Owners",
    "text": "2. If the investor is an entity that was formed for the purpose of purchasing a Note, each of its beneficial owners meets one of the criteria described in clauses (i)-(vi) of paragraph 1; and"
  },
  {
    "id": "c218",
    "name": "3. Investment Intent",
    "text": "3. The investor is acquiring the Note solely for the investor’s own account and not directly or indirectly for the account of any other person whatsoever (or, if the investor is acquiring the Note as a trustee, solely for the account of the trust or trust account identified) for investment and not with a view to, or for sale in connection with, any distribution of the Note. The investor does not have any contract, undertaking or arrangement with any person to sell, transfer or grant a participation to any person with respect to the Note; and"
  },
  {
    "id": "c219",
    "name": "4. Investor Sophistication and Risk Capacity",
    "text": "4, The investor has such knowledge and experience in financial and business matters that the investor is capable of evaluating the merits and risks of the investment evidenced by the investor’s purchase of the Note, and the Investor is able to bear the economic risk of such investment."
  },
  {
    "id": "c220",
    "name": "Manager’s Subscription Discretion",
    "text": "Prospective investors are reminded that, notwithstanding his qualification as a suitable purchaser of Notes, the Manager may accept or reject, in its sole and absolute discretion, all or a portion of such investor’s subscription for a Note or subscription to increase the principal amount of a Note."
  },
  {
    "id": "c221",
    "name": "IX. Additional Information — Questions and Information",
    "text": "IX. Additional Information Prior to the closing of the offering, the Company will provide to each prospective Noteholder and such Noteholder’s representatives and advisors, if any, the opportunity to ask questions and receive answers concerning the terms and conditions of this offering and to obtain any additional information which the Company may possess, or can obtain without unreasonable effort or expense, that is necessary to verify the accuracy of the information furnished to such prospective investor. Any such questions should be directed to the Principals. No other persons have been authorized to give information or to make any representations concerning this offering, and if given or made, such other information or representations must not be relied upon as having been authorized by the Company."
  },
  {
    "id": "c222",
    "name": "Memorandum Qualification by Note Documents",
    "text": "This Memorandum is intended to present a general outline of the policies and structure of the Company and its Managing Member, and the terms of the Notes. The Notes and the Note Purchase Agreement, which specify the rights and obligations of the Noteholders, should be reviewed thoroughly by each prospective purchaser of Notes. The Summary of Principal Terms contained herein is necessarily incomplete and is qualified in its entirety by reference to the Note Purchase Agreement."
  },
  {
    "id": "c223",
    "name": "Direction of Inquiries",
    "text": "Inquiries relating to this Memorandum or to a possible purchase of Notes should be directed to: Mortgage Fund ’08 LLC c/o The Mortgage Fund LLC Attention: Kelly Ng or Walter Ng, Managing Directors Telephone: (925) 282-1020 201 Lafayette Circle Lafayette, CA 94549 To Kelly Ng at kelly@bar-kmortgage.com"
  }
]

